  "answer": "### Short Answer\nYes, the employee is required to file an annual Income Tax Return (ITR) if they had two employers during the year. This is mandated under Section 51 of the National Internal Revenue Code (NIRC).\n\n### Controlling Authorities\n- **NIRC Section 51**: \"An individual deriving compensation concurrently from two or more employers at any time during the taxable year shall file an income tax return.\"\n\n### Interpretation\nThe provision clearly states that individuals who receive compensation from multiple employers within the same taxable year must file an ITR. This requirement applies regardless of the total income amount, as the law aims to ensure that all income is reported and taxed appropriately.\n\n### Practical Meaning\nFor the employee, this means they must prepare and submit an ITR detailing their total income from both employers. Failure to comply could result in penalties or issues with the Bureau of Internal Revenue (BIR). It is advisable for the employee to gather all necessary documentation, such as certificates of withholding tax from each employer, to accurately report their income and taxes withheld.",
